### 2023NatAs...7...80U: Simultaneous radio and optical polarimetry of GRB 191221B afterglow (2023; transients; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Is the radio (97.5 GHz) polarization of the GRB 191221B afterglow depolarized relative to the optical, and what does this say about magnetic field configuration and cool electrons / jet energy?
- step 3: Model afterglow light curves and SED
  - method: Numerical forward-shock synchrotron model with Blandford-McKee solution, equal arrival time surface, fixed theta_j; adjust parameters to fit radio-optical-X-ray data [Methods: "We performed numerical calculations of flux from the shock with a fixed $\theta_j$ which evolves as the Blandford-McKee (BM) self-similar solution"]
  - assumption: Uniform density medium, energy injection E_iso propto t^0.25 [Methods: "the ambient density is uniform and the energy continues to be injected to the shock by long activity of the central engine"]
  - validation: Model also explains X-ray and optical [Introduction: "The model also explains the X-ray and optical afterglows"]
- result: 97.5 GHz light curve break time = 3.77 ± 0.35 days [Introduction: "a break-time at $3.77\pm 0.35$ days"]
- result: Pre-break index alpha = 0.26 ± 0.02 dimensionless [Introduction: "$\alpha = 0.26\pm 0.02$ (before the break)"]
- result: Post-break index alpha = -1.62 ± 0.13 dimensionless [Introduction: "$\alpha = -1.62\pm 0.13$ (after the break)"]
- limitation (authors): PA difference between radio and optical only ~90% significant [Methods: "the PA between radio and optical is different at $\sim$90% confidence level."]
- not stated (card): tclean parameters: weighting/robust, cell size, image size, masks; Self-calibration solution intervals; Beam sizes and image rms values; Fitting method/priors for the afterglow model and light curve broken power law; Numerical afterglow polarization code is not public; ALMA configurations and on-source times; Source coordinates

### 2017ApJ...844..123F: The Circumgalactic Medium of Submillimeter Galaxies. II. Unobscured QSOs within Dusty Starbursts and QSO Sightlines with Impact Parameters below 100 kpc (2017; high_z_galaxies, agn_and_quasars; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Are Herschel-selected SMGs near QSOs (5-30 arcsec) true projected SMG-QSO pairs, or are the QSOs hosted by SMGs, and what are the properties of the composites?
- step 5: QSO correction of IR photometry via SED templates
  - method: Fit QSO template to SDSS+WISE photometry (plus torus for G15 1450+0026), extrapolate to far-IR to subtract QSO contribution [4.1.1 QSO Contribution to the IR Emissi…: "we can fit the SDSS and WISE photometry with a QSO SED template and then estimate the QSO correction in the far-IR by extrapolating the best-fit QSO template."]
  - parameter: Torus: QSO template reddened by A_V = 4 mag with Calzetti extinction law [4.1.1 QSO Contribution to the IR Emissi…: "it is constructed by reddening the QSO template by $A_V = 4$ mag with the (cite: Calzetti00) extinction law."]
  - assumption: Negligible dust-obscured star formation at rest-frame <10 um [4.1.1 QSO Contribution to the IR Emissi…: "By assuming negligible contribution from dust-obscured star formation at $\lambda_{\rm rest} < 10$ $\mu$m"]
  - validation: Uncertainty at least 0.13 dex from dispersion of QSO SEDs [4.1.1 QSO Contribution to the IR Emissi…: "The uncertainty of these estimates is at least 0.13 dex, which is the dispersion of individual QSO SEDs around the mean SED."]
- step 6: Derive gas/dust mass and surface density
  - method: Extrapolate observed 870 um luminosity to rest-frame 850 um with modified blackbody; apply L850/Mgas ratio and gas-to-dust ratio [4.1.2 Mass and Surface Density of the I…: "we extrapolate the *observed* 870 $\mu$m luminosity to *rest-frame* 850 $\mu$m luminosity using a modified blackbody with $T = 25$ K, $\beta = 1.8$, and $\lambda_0 = 50$ $\mu$m."]
  - parameter: L850/Mgas = (6.7±1.7)e19 erg/s/Hz/Msun; Mgas/Mdust = 140 [4.1.2 Mass and Surface Density of the I…: "$L_\nu^{850}/M_{\rm gas} = (6.7\pm1.7)\times10^{19}$ erg s$^{-1}$ Hz$^{-1}$ $M_\odot^{-1}$"]
  - assumption: Galactic X_CO = 3e20 (alpha_CO = 6.5); alpha_CO=1.4 alternative [4.1.2 Mass and Surface Density of the I…: "the Rayleigh-Jeans method assumes the Galactic CO-to-H$_2$ conversion factor, $X_{\rm CO} \equiv N_{\rm H_2}/W_{\rm CO} = 3\times10^{20}$"]
- step 7: Fit IR luminosity and SFR
  - method: Fit modified blackbody (optically thick, lambda0 = 150 um, beta = 2) to SPIRE+ALMA photometry; integrate 8-1000 um; SFR = L_IR/1e10 (Kennicutt98, Chabrier IMF) [4.1.3 IR Luminosity and SFR Surface Den…: "We fixed $\lambda_0$ at 150 $\mu$m for a typical power-law slope of $\beta = 2$"]
  - parameter: Redshift fixed (QSO z for composites, 2.5 otherwise) [4.1 Properties of the QSO Host Galaxies: "assume a redshift of 2.5 for the remaining ALMA sources"]
  - assumption: Constant dust temperature, local thermal equilibrium [4.1.3 IR Luminosity and SFR Surface Den…: "assuming local thermal equilibrium at a constant temperature $T$"]
  - fitting: Modified blackbody fit; Monte Carlo simulations of synthetic photometry for statistical error [4.1.3 IR Luminosity and SFR Surface Den…: "through Monte Carlo simulations of synthetic *Herschel* and ALMA photometry"]
  - validation: Compared with optically thin models and Arp 220 SED; L_IR differs by ~0.1 dex [4.1.3 IR Luminosity and SFR Surface Den…: "The resulting $L_{\rm IR}$ can be higher than the blackbody fits by $\sim$0.1 dex"]
- result: Mean SFR surface density of SMG-QSO composites = ~260 ± not stated Msun/yr/kpc^2 [4.1.3 IR Luminosity and SFR Surface Den…: "The mean SFR surface density of the SMG-QSO composites is $\sim$260 $M_{\odot}$ yr$^{-1}$ kpc$^{-2}$."]
- result: Mean dust mass surface density (alpha_CO=6.5) = ~8e8 ± not stated Msun/kpc^2 [4.1.2 Mass and Surface Density of the I…: "the average dust mass surface density is $\sim8\times10^8$ $M_{\odot}$ kpc$^{-2}$"]
- limitation (authors): Redshifts of non-QSO SMGs unknown (assumed 2.5), making their results less reliable [4.1.3 IR Luminosity and SFR Surface Den…: "The results for the rest of the sample are less reliable because of their unknown redshifts"]
- limitation (authors): Size measurements at SNR<10 are biased high [4.1.2 Mass and Surface Density of the I…: "Note that the size estimates are biased high at ${\rm SNR} < 10$"]
- limitation (authors): Duty cycle is an upper limit since no BH accretion assumed in SMGs without QSOs [4.3 Star-Formation-AGN Co-evolution at …: "This estimate of the QSO duty cycle should be considered as an upper limit"]
- not stated (card): Number of clean iterations / niter and the exact mask definition procedure; Whether continuum was formed from all four spws without line flagging; Details of the SED fitting code, fitting method and priors (modified blackbody fit procedure); QSO template fit procedure to SDSS/WISE photometry; Stacking procedure details for undetected QSOs; Bootstrapping details for mean size; CASA version used fo…

### 2020A&A...642A.155Z: GOODS-ALMA: Optically dark ALMA galaxies shed light on a cluster in formation at z = 3.5 (2020; high_z_galaxies, galaxy_clusters_and_sz; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What are the properties of the six optically dark galaxies in the GOODS-ALMA survey, what are their redshifts, and do they trace a proto-cluster at z~3.5?
- step 2: Determine spectroscopic redshifts of AGS4 and AGS17
  - method: Identify the single line as CO(6-5) using SED/PDF of photometric redshift, far-IR SED dust temperature, L_IR-L_CO relation, [CI] non-detection, and redshift-peak probability [3.2 AGS17: "we decided to favor the CO(6-5) transition from a purely probabilistic point of view."]
  - assumption: Typical dust temperature ~38-40 K at these redshifts (Schreiber18) [3.2 AGS17: "close to the typical dust temperature $T_{\rm dust}$ $\sim$ 40 K at $z$ = 3.5 (cite: Schreiber18)"]
  - validation: consistency with photometric redshift PDF and 4000 A break [3.1 AGS4: "gives a $z_{spec}^{AGS4}$ = 3.566, which agrees with the 4000 Å discontinuity in the optical SED."]
- step 3: Deblend optical/NIR photometry and derive photometric redshifts and stellar masses
  - method: De-blend neighbors, fit SEDs with EAzY (redshift) and FAST++ (mass; delayed exponentially declining SFH, Calzetti attenuation) [2.4.2: "derived from the observed optical to mid-infrared SED fitted with `FAST++` assuming a delayed exponentially declining SFH and a (cite: Calzetti00) attenuation law"]
  - software: EAzY, FAST++, qdeblend
  - assumption: Salpeter IMF; H0=70, Om=0.3, OL=0.7 [1 Introduction: "We use cosmological parameters of H$_{0}$ = 70 km s$^{-1}$ Mpc$^{-1}$, $\Omega_{M}$ = 0.3, and $\Omega_{\Lambda}$ = 0.7."]
  - validation: AGS24 mass tested with varied SFH and attenuation laws (FAST++, CIGALE); stays within 10^11.31-10^11.52 [5.1: "the stellar mass of AGS24 remains within 10$^{11.31}$ and 10$^{11.52}$ M$_{\odot}$"]
- step 4: Derive infrared luminosities and SFRs, and test for radio AGN
  - method: CIGALE fit to Herschel+ALMA for AGS4/17; Schreiber18 templates to 1.1 mm for others; SFR_IR from Kennicutt98; q_TIR and L1.4GHz/SFR vs radio-excess threshold [2.4.3: "Infrared luminosities are measured by the SED fitting of the *Herschel* and ALMA flux densities for AGS4 and AGS17 using `CIGALE`"]
  - software: CIGALE
  - parameter: radio spectral index alpha = -0.8 [2.4.4: "assuming a radio spectral index of $\alpha$ = $\alpha^{\rm 3GHz}_{\rm 1.4GHz}$ = $-0.8$"]
  - validation: AGS24 L_IR checked with Draine 2007 SED within 10% [5.1: "which gives the same result within 10%"]
- result: z_spec AGS4 = 3.556 ± not stated dimensionless [abstract: "$z^{\rm AGS4}_{spec}$ = 3.556"]
- result: z_spec AGS17 = 3.467 ± not stated dimensionless [abstract: "$z^{\rm AGS17}_{spec}$ = 3.467"]
- result: AGS24 stellar mass = 10^11.32 ± +0.02/-0.19 dex log Msun [5.1: "the stellar mass of AGS24 is *M$_{\star}$* = 10$^{11.32^{+0.02}_{-0.19}}$ M$_{\odot}$"]
- result: AGS4 stellar mass (z=3.556) = 10^11.09 ± ±0.08 dex log Msun [4.1: "the stellar mass of AGS4 is *M*$_\star$ = 10$^{11.09\pm0.08}$ M$_\odot$"]
- result: AGS4 q_TIR = 2.49 ± ±0.06 dimensionless [4.1: "$q_{\rm TIR}$ = 2.49$\pm$0.06"]
- limitation (authors): Single lines cannot give definitive redshifts [3.1 AGS4: "One ALMA spectral line alone is not sufficient to obtain a definitive spectroscopic redshift for AGS4."]
- not stated (card): tclean/imaging parameters for the band 4 continuum and cubes (weighting, cell, size, robust); Line-finding algorithm, S/N definition and line-free channels in GILDAS; CASA/pipeline version; Flux calibrator and flux-scale uncertainty; Photometric deblending parameters and FAST++/EAzY grids; Details of the Monte Carlo (0.4%) probability and overdensity random samples; CO luminosity conversion used …

### 2014ApJ...788..125S: An ALMA Survey of Submillimeter Galaxies in the Extended Chandra Deep Field South: The Redshift Distribution and Evolution of Submillimeter Galaxies (2014; high_z_galaxies; multi_facility_alma_minor)
- question: What is the photometric redshift distribution, stellar mass distribution and evolutionary fate of ALMA-identified 870 um SMGs in the ECDFS?
- step 2: Derive photometric redshifts by SED fitting
  - method: hyperz chi2 fitting of Bruzual & Charlot templates with four SFHs, z=0-7, A_V 0-5, Calzetti law [3 Photometric redshifts: "We use the spectral templates of (cite: Bruzual03), with solar metalicities, and consider four SFHs; a single burst (B), constant star formation (C) and two exponentially decaying SFHs"]
  - software: hyperz
  - parameter: minimum 4 detected bands required [3.2.1 Reliability of SMG redshifts: "We therefore can be confident in the reliability of photometric redshifts derived from detections in four photometric bands, and adopt this limit throughout our analysis."]
  - assumption: Calzetti dust law; galaxy age less than age of Universe [3 Photometric redshifts: "We also include the constraint that the age of the galaxy must be less than the age of Universe."]
  - fitting: minimum chi2; 99% hyperz intervals adopted as 1 sigma errors [3.1 Training sample: "we determine that the Hyperz “99 per cent” confidence intervals provide the best estimate of the redshift error"]
  - validation: Photometric offsets calibrated on 5942 spectroscopic galaxies; compared to 22 ALESS spectroscopic redshifts [3.1 Training sample: "measuring a median $\Delta \,z\,$ / $( 1+z_{\rm spec} ) = 0.011\,\pm \,0.002$"]
- step 4: Stellar masses
  - method: M_H from best-fit SED times M/L_H from burst and constant SFH Bruzual & Charlot models, averaged; Salpeter IMF [4.3 Stellar Masses: "We note that all the stellar masses quoted here are for a Salpeter Initial Mass Function (IMF)"]
  - software: Starburst99
  - assumption: SFH age restricted to 20 Myr–1 Gyr [Footnote 9: "We enforce the condition that the age of the star-formation event is $20$ Myr $\le$ $t_{\rm age}$ $\le$ $1$ Gyr."]
  - validation: Compared to dynamical and gas masses of similar SMGs [4.3 Stellar Masses: "we can crudely test their accuracy by comparing them to the dynamical masses, and CO–derived gas masses, of similar SMGs."]
- result: Median photometric redshift of 77 SMGs = 2.3 ± 0.1 dimensionless [abstract: "deriving a median photometric redshift of $z_{phot}\,=\,2.3\,\pm\,0.1$"]
- result: Median stellar mass = 8e10 ± 1e10 Msun [4.3 Stellar Masses: "median stellar mass for the 96 ALESS SMGs of $M_{\star} = (8 \pm 1) \times 10^{10}$ M$_{\odot}$"]
- limitation (authors): Stellar masses have systematic uncertainties up to 5x because SFHs are unconstrained [abstract: "although there are systematic uncertainties of up to 5 $\times$ for individual sources"]
- not stated (card): ALMA calibration and imaging settings (all in Hodge et al. 2013); Zibetti et al. HAWK-I data are unpublished; Exact hyperz configuration beyond the listed parameters; Details of the clipped-mean stacking algorithm and mask choices; Number of bootstrap realisations

### 2024A&A...690A..55S: NOEMA formIng Cluster survEy (NICE): Characterizing eight massive galaxy groups at 1.5 &lt; z &lt; 4 in the COSMOS field (2024; high_z_galaxies, galaxy_clusters_and_sz; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Spectroscopically confirm eight massive galaxy group candidates in COSMOS at 1.5<z<4, and measure their integrated SFR, dark matter halo masses and baryonic accretion rates to test whether they are forming clusters
- step 2: Detect lines and determine redshifts
  - method: Line search of highest S/N lines; power-law continuum with slope 3.7; Gaussian fit with MPFIT; single-line redshifts resolved by comparing with COSMOS2020 photo-z PDF(z) [4.1 Line detection and redshift determi…: "The continuum-subtracted spectra are then fitted with a Gaussian line-profile using `MPFIT`"]
  - software: MPFIT, LePhare
  - parameter: P_chance<5% for masking lines; robust lines sigma>7, p_chance<0.001 [5.1 Emission lines and redshifts: "We detect robust ($\sigma>7$,$p_{\rm chance}<0.001$) emission lines in seven out of the eight pointings"]
  - assumption: Modified blackbody with beta~1.7 [4.1 Line detection and redshift determi…: "assuming modified blackbody emission with $\beta\sim1.7$ (cite: Magdis2012SED)"]
  - fitting: Gaussian line fit with MPFIT [4.1 Line detection and redshift determi…: "The continuum-subtracted spectra are then fitted with a Gaussian line-profile using `MPFIT`[^3], at the frequencies identified by the line searching algorithm."]
  - validation: 2023.1.00652.S [CI] observation confirmed one source identified from PDF(z) comparison [4.1 Line detection and redshift determi…: "which further validates our identification method"]
- step 4: Integrated FIR photometry and SED fit
  - method: Super-deblending with one SCUBA2 peak prior per group, PSF fitting of Herschel/SCUBA2/MeerKAT maps; STARDUST SED fit; radio extrapolated from IR-radio relation [4.3 Integrated FIR spectral energy dist…: "we fit the integrated FIR spectral energy distribution (SED) using `STARDUST` (cite: Kokorev2021)"]
  - software: STARDUST
  - parameter: Exclude sources within 10'' of prior; >3 sigma detections, otherwise 3 sigma upper limits [4.3 Integrated FIR spectral energy dist…: "Only photometric points with a significant detection ($>3\sigma$) were considered in the fitting"]
  - assumption: Dust templates of Magdis2012; optically thin dust mass [5.2 Integrated FIR properties: "based on dust templates from (cite: Magdis2012SED)"]
  - fitting: STARDUST fit at group zspec [4.3 Integrated FIR spectral energy dist…: "The fitting was performed at the spectroscopic redshift of each group"]
  - validation: Residual maps checked; fluxes calibrated by Monte Carlo simulations [4.3 Integrated FIR spectral energy dist…: "the measured fluxes and their uncertainties are then calibrated by Monte Carlo simulations performed on the maps"]
- result: Spectroscopic redshift range of the eight groups = 1.65-3.61 ± not stated dimensionless [abstract: "eight massive galaxy groups at redshifts $1.65\leq z\leq3.61$"]
- result: Integrated IR SFR range = 262-1319 ± not stated Msun/yr [5.2 Integrated FIR properties: "${\rm SFR_{IR}}=(262-1319)\,{\rm M_{\odot}/yr}$"]
- result: Radio-loud AGN fraction in NICE = 2/9 (22%) ± not stated dimensionless [5.2 Integrated FIR properties: "this gives a radio-loud AGN fraction of 2/9 (i.e. 22%) in the NICE sample"]
- limitation (authors): Halo mass estimates carry large uncertainties; group centre and concentration degeneracies; interloper contamination [5.4 Baryonic accretion rate: "Given that the dark matter halo estimates are prone to large uncertainties"]
- limitation (authors): Cold-stream regime scatter based on only six points [6.3 Baryonic accretion: Flattening or n…: "This increased observed scatter is only from six points and would require a larger sample to confirm"]
- not stated (card): GILDAS/uvfit and mapping parameters and ALMA/NOEMA calibrator and flux-scale details; Line-search threshold implementation and exact line-free channel masks; STARDUST fit configuration and priors beyond the Magdis2012 templates; Stellar mass completeness limits used per group and the numerical stellar mass cuts; Group centre definition parameters, NFW fit grid ranges and prior ranges; Exact super…

